public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[] temps = {100.0, 37.0, -40.0};
        for (double c : temps) {
            System.out.printf("%.1f C = %.1f F%n", c, celsiusToF(c));
        }

        int[] years = {2024, 1900, 2000, 2023};
        for (int y : years) {
            System.out.println(y + " leap: " + isLeapYear(y));
        }

        int[] scores = {95, 83, 74, 61, 40};
        for (int s : scores) {
            System.out.println("Score " + s + ": " + grade(s));
        }
    }

    static double celsiusToF(double c) {
        return c * 9 / 5 + 32;
    }

    static boolean isLeapYear(int y) {
        if (y % 400 == 0) {
            return true;
        }
        if (y % 100 == 0) {
            return false;
        }
        return y % 4 == 0;
    }

    static char grade(int score) {
        if (score >= 90) {
            return 'A';
        }
        if (score >= 80) {
            return 'B';
        }
        if (score >= 70) {
            return 'C';
        }
        if (score >= 60) {
            return 'D';
        }
        return 'F';
    }
}

steps.body:
**Overloading** means several methods share a name but differ in their parameter types or count. Java picks the one whose parameters match the arguments at compile time:

```java
static String describe(int n) { ... }
static String describe(String s) { ... }

describe(42);    // the int version
describe("42");  // the String version
```

Only the parameter list counts. Two methods that differ just in return type are a compile error. Watch for widening: `describe('A')` has no `char` version, so the char widens to `int` and you get the int version with 65.

**Varargs** let a method take any number of arguments of one type. Write `...` after the type:

```java
static int sum(int... nums) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int n : nums) {
        total += n;
    }
    return total;
}
```

Inside the method, `nums` is just an `int[]`, so use `nums.length`, `nums[i]`, and loops as usual. Callers may pass zero values (`sum()` gets an empty array, never `null`), several values separated by commas, or an existing `int[]`. A method has at most one varargs parameter, and it must come last: `format(String label, int... nums)`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(describe(42));
        System.out.println(describe("42"));

        System.out.println("sum() = " + sum());
        System.out.println("sum(3, 4) = " + sum(3, 4));
        System.out.println("sum(1, 2, 3, 4) = " + sum(1, 2, 3, 4));

        int[] scores = {10, 20, 30};
        System.out.println("sum(scores) = " + sum(scores));
    }

    static String describe(int n) {
        return "int " + n + (n % 2 == 0 ? " (even)" : " (odd)");
    }

    static String describe(String s) {
        return "String \"" + s + "\" with " + s.length() + " chars";
    }

    static int sum(int... nums) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) {
            total += n;
        }
        return total;
    }
}

